module Darklang.Cli.Docs.Escapes

// A raw string can't hold three double quotes, so the page writes them as three
// single quotes and swaps them in at the end.
let content(): String =
  """# String escapes

A backslash in a "..." string or a '...' char starts an escape.

## Single characters
  \n   newline (10)            \0   NUL (0)
  \t   tab (9)                 \\   backslash
  \r   carriage return (13)    \"   double quote
  \a   bell (7)                \'   single quote
  \b   backspace (8)           \/   slash
  \v   vertical tab (11)       \{   {
  \f   form feed (12)          \}   }

  "name:\tJo\n"    a tab, then a newline at the end

## Code points
  \xHH         2 hex digits    "\x41" is "A"
  \uHHHH       4 hex digits    "\u00e9" is "é"
  \XHHHH       the same as \u
  \UHHHHHHHH   8 hex digits    "\U0001F600" is "😀"

Hex digits may be upper or lower case. Each escape names a Unicode code point,
not a byte: "\xff" is "ÿ", which UTF-8 stores as the two bytes 195 191. To
write raw bytes, use `Stdlib.Bytes.fromList([ 255u8 ])`.

There are no octal escapes. `\0` is NUL on its own, so "\012" is NUL followed
by "12".

## Mistakes are errors
  "a\qb"   error[PARSE-ESCAPE]: Invalid escape sequence or codepoint in string literal

An unknown letter, too few hex digits ("\x4") and a code point that isn't a
Unicode scalar value (a surrogate like "\uD800", or anything above
"\U0010FFFF") are all errors. The backslash is never kept as a literal; write
\\ for one.

## Chars
  '\n'   '\''   '\x41'   '\u00e9'

Char literals take the same escapes.

## Interpolated strings
  $"total:\t{x}\n"    escapes work as in "..."
  $"{{x}} is {x}"     {{ and }} are literal braces: "{x} is 5"
  $"\{x\} is {x}"     and so are \{ and \}

## Raw strings
  '''a\nb'''          no escapes: 4 chars, a \ n b
  $'''{x}\n'''        interpolates, but \n stays as typed

Use them for text full of backslashes, such as regexes and Windows paths.
`docs syntax` has the other string forms."""
  |> Stdlib.String.replaceAll("'''", "\"\"\"")
